  <h3>Run the 10% and 20% test before you pay</h3>

  <p>
    College is one part of career success, often a small part. A planning rule: keep the college fee near 10% of the whole education budget, and treat anything above about 20% as a serious caution that needs course-specific proof. These are planning heuristics, not laws. The rest of the budget pays for living costs, tools, books, projects, courses and a safety cushion.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>At 10% of the budget</th>
          <th>At 20% of the budget</th>
          <th>Our read</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {budgetRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{item.budget}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="At 10% of the budget">{item.tenPct}</td>
            <td data-label="At 20% of the budget">{item.twentyPct}</td>
            <td data-label="Our read">{item.verdict}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Spending more can be justified only with evidence for this exact course:</strong> a current syllabus, working labs or simulators, internships with named operators, and a placement record that shows how many of the whole batch got offers, in which roles and at what pay. A big employer's name on a poster is not that evidence. Avoid education debt, because oil pay can pause just when repayments start.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Learning is partly free. Good YouTube lectures, university open courseware and society resources teach reservoir and drilling basics. Check each course for the instructor's field experience, last update, exercises and feedback. A pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio, but a decline-curve analysis on public well data, shared with a short write-up, is.
  </p>

  <h2 id="oil-cycle">The oil-price cycle and layoffs</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part brochures skip. Oil is a cyclical industry, and the people who joined just before a crash paid for it.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>After the price fall that began in late 2014, the industry cut deeply between Fall 2014 and Spring 2016. A 2019 Society of Petroleum Engineers column said the industry may never fully recover those jobs and that enrolment in petroleum engineering had fallen markedly.</li>
    <li>In 2020, prices fell from above $60 a barrel to below $25, and US crude briefly went negative in April. Halliburton, for example, reported a headcount near 50,000 by its April filing, down from about 55,000 at the start of the year.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    India's public-sector employers are slower to cut, but the cycle still reaches them through smaller hiring waves. Look at who feels a crash first.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Who</th>
          <th>How fast</th>
          <th>Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {cycleRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Who"><strong>{item.who}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="How fast">{item.speed}</td>
            <td data-label="Why">{item.why}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The plan that survives a crash has three parts: savings of several months of expenses, a skill that works outside oil (data, carbon storage, geothermal, project controls), and a network that includes people in other industries. Build all three in the first years while the pay is good.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ongc-route">ONGC, GATE and the PSU route</h2>

  <p>
    For most graduates, ONGC and Oil India are the dream employers. The route has several stages, and competition at each one.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">01</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Stage one: a degree that matches the post</div>
        <p>Graduate trainee posts are at the E1 level. Production, drilling and cementing posts in petroleum ask for a petroleum degree. Other posts accept mechanical or chemical engineering, and reservoir posts also accept geology, geophysics and related science degrees. All ask for at least 60% in the qualifying degree.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">02</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Stage two: GATE or the company's own test</div>
        <p>In GATE cycles, the Careers360 explainer lists a merit formula of 60 marks for GATE, 25 for the degree and 15 for the interview. The 2026 notice (Advt. 2/2026) reported by several portals used a computer-based test instead, with interview and medical after it. It lists 52 engineering and geology posts in all, and one portal lists only 4 of them for petroleum. We could not check the official PDF, so confirm the numbers on ongcindia.com.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="checkpoint">
      <div class="checkpoint__num">03</div>
      <div class="checkpoint__body">
        <div class="checkpoint__title">Stage three: the later promotion filter</div>
        <p>Getting in is one filter. Moving into senior asset-manager roles is another, with merit, vacancies and experience all mattering. This is the multi-stage competition that makes the route Not so scalable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>What this means for choosing a branch:</strong> a petroleum degree is not the only key to ONGC. Many students do better by studying mechanical, chemical or geology at a strong government college, preparing for GATE in the paper that matches their degree, and keeping oil and gas as one of several targets. Check the GATE paper code and the post list in each year's notice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="field-life">Offshore and field life, and safety</h2>

  <p>
    Ask what a normal month looks like before you commit. Drilling and production roles include time at a rig, a platform or a well site. Reservoir and data roles are mostly desk-based. Rotation patterns, travel and posting locations differ by employer and by role, so ask for the exact posting in writing.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What the work can ask of you</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Remote postings away from family and big cities.</li>
        <li>Shift and rotation schedules, and being on call during well problems.</li>
        <li>Strict medical standards (ONGC's notices require them) and safety training.</li>
        <li>Weather and sea conditions for offshore work.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">How to test it before you commit</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Speak to a working drilling or production engineer, not only a recruiter.</li>
        <li>Ask about the last year's incidents, training and rest policy.</li>
        <li>Look at the 2005 Bombay High North platform fire, which destroyed the platform in under two hours and led to a high-level inquiry, as a reminder that offshore safety is a serious subject.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="pay">Pay, from named sources only</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where</th>
          <th>Figure</th>
          <th>How to read it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where"><strong>{item.source}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Figure">{item.figure}</td>
            <td data-label="How to read it">{item.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings (for example on LinkedIn, Naukri or Indeed) and ask people working in the role before you decide.
  </p>

  <p>
    One more point on pay: a high number in a boom year is not the same as a high lifetime income. A career with two or three lean years in a decade can end up behind a steadier one, so compare total earning over a decade, not the best year.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transition">Energy transition: what is real</h2>

  <p>
    Subsurface skills do transfer to cleaner energy. The honest picture is that the transfer is real but small today.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Geothermal</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">ONGC's Puga pilot in Ladakh</div>
      <p>A demonstration project with two wells of about 1,000 metres and an initial target of one megawatt, run with the Ladakh administration. Drilling had a two-year setback and a five-year collaboration was renewed in June 2026, according to press reports.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Carbon capture and storage</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Pilot studies, not a job lane yet</div>
      <p>ONGC describes carbon capture, use and storage as a lever in its plan to reach net zero for its own emissions by 2038. Pilots assess storage rock and use cases. Reservoir engineers are the natural fit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Hydrogen and ammonia</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">A roadmap, with progress unverified</div>
      <p>ONGC has an internal hydrogen roadmap. We found no recent public update on progress, so do not plan a career around it yet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Global view</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">New roles are expected</div>
      <p>The S&P Global and EAGE review expects new roles in carbon capture and storage, geothermal and digital work. It also notes that exploration drilling and discoveries may have hit record lows in 2024.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Hire numbers for these roles are not published, so treat them as a direction for your skills, not a guaranteed job.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and the digital oilfield</h2>

  <p>
    AI is already changing how wells are planned, and it is a real leverage opportunity. The S&P Global and EAGE review expects AI-driven models to improve subsurface imaging and reduce exploration risk, and AI-powered automation to tune drilling parameters. A 2019 SPE column said drilling engineers may increasingly resemble data scientists with a drilling background.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What is under pressure</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Routine log interpretation and report drafting.</li>
        <li>Manual monitoring of well data that sensors and software can watch.</li>
        <li>Fixed-recipe drilling parameter choices.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What stays human</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Judging whether a model's answer makes physical sense.</li>
        <li>Safety decisions and well control.</li>
        <li>Explaining risk and cost to a manager or partner.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A staged path: start with Python, spreadsheets and a plotting library on public well data. Then learn a reservoir or drilling simulation tool through the free tiers your institute offers. Later, learn to check and challenge model output, and to automate repeated reports. The bigger disruption risk for this career is the price cycle and the energy transition, with AI as a third pressure that mainly raises the bar for entry roles.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skill-stack">The skill stack that survives a crash</h2>

  <p>
    A degree gets you into the room. A <strong>high-income skill portfolio</strong> decides how fast you grow once you are in it, and how well you cope when oil falls. Build it in three layers.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Core skill</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">One subsurface or wells specialty</div>
      <p>Reservoir engineering, well testing or drilling design. Go deep in one, not shallow in all four.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Multiplier skill</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">Data and Python, plus clear English</div>
      <p>Data skill lets you work in other industries. Clear writing and speaking get you the project lead role over an equally skilled colleague.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Visible proof</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">A public-data project and a short write-up</div>
      <p>One finished analysis, explained simply, is stronger than five course certificates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Network</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">People outside your own employer</div>
      <p>Professional society student chapters, alumni and field engineers you ask good questions of. Referrals decide who gets the next job after a layoff.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Think big about where the skill can go. A reservoir engineer who also reads data well can move into carbon storage, geothermal, groundwater or mining. An engineer who only knows one employer's software is stuck when that employer stops hiring.
  </p>

  <h2 id="growth">Routes ranked by growth headroom</h2>

  <p>
    We lead with the routes that can grow beyond one employer's pay scale. Crowded or capped routes stay in the list, marked with their risk.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Rating</th>
          <th>Why</th>
