            <td data-label="Business layer">{item.layer}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> an independent consultancy, or a well-testing, inspection or audit firm, scales because you set the price, take repeat work from several clients and can later hire junior engineers. It needs years of proven work first, so treat it as a second-decade goal, not a first-job plan.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="business-layer">Who pays, and how they find you</h2>

  <p>
    In an employed job, your employer pays and a recruiter or a test finds you. In consulting or services, the payer is a small operator, a service company, a contractor or a project owner who needs a specific problem solved.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>How they find and trust you:</strong> referrals from former colleagues and managers come first. Proof comes second: a short technical note, a case write-up that removes confidential detail, or a conference paper.</li>
    <li><strong>First skill worth learning:</strong> positioning. Be able to say in two sentences who you help and with what, for example "I help small operators check decline forecasts on old wells."</li>
    <li><strong>Then:</strong> a simple offer (one clear deliverable and a price), follow-up after every project, and a polite ask for an introduction.</li>
    <li><strong>One number to watch:</strong> how many technical conversations turn into a paid first project.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If you take the fixed public-sector route, this layer matters mainly for your side route or your move after leaving. Rules on outside work differ by employer, so check them first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="alternatives">Better adjacent options</h2>

  <p>
    For most students, one of these four beats a petroleum degree on breadth, price or both. If you still want oil and gas, they keep that door open.
  </p>

